    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "I've implemented `check_server(server, cases)` in `mcpcheck/inproc.py`, and all 4 tests pass (the 2 existing ones and 2 new ones).\n\n**How it works:**\n- It opens `mcp.Client(server, mode=\"legacy\")`. The mcp SDK then runs the server as a background task and connects to it through in-memory streams, so there's no subprocess and no socket. It also does the normal `initialize` handshake first.\n- It calls each case's tool in order with `client.session.call_tool(...)`, which is the same `ClientSession.call_tool` that `check_command` uses. It checks each result with the same `compare()` from `mcpcheck/cases.py` and collects mismatches in the same order. So the protocol path and the comparison match the stdio version.\n- I used `mode=\"legacy\"` on purpose. The default `\"auto\"` mode, for a server running in the same process, skips the handshake and the JSON-RPC message format and calls the server directly. That would test a different path from the one `check_command` exercises over stdio.\n- `server` is typed as `MCPServer`, the class behind the high-level decorator API (it was called `FastMCP` before mcp 2.x). No dependencies were added.\n\n**Tests:** I added `tests/test_inproc.py`, which uses the anyio pytest plugin that comes with mcp. It builds a small `MCPServer` with `add` and `greet` tools and checks two things:\n- A mix of passing and failing cases gives back exactly the failing ones, in order, with the text the tool actually returned.\n- A run where every case passes returns `[]`.\n\nA server author would use it like this:\n```python\nmismatches = await check_server(my_server, load_cases(\"cases.toml\"))\nassert mismatches == []\n```"
  },
